# Line endings are a property of the REPOSITORY, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (DBT-71). Without it, how a file is stored and checked out
# depends on each clone's `core.autocrlf`, and two defects shipped from that in
# a single day - both of them green through CI:
#
#   DBT-66  check-listings.mjs carried a shebang. Under CRLF vitest could not
#           import it, collapsing that whole suite to "no tests" - not one
#           failing test, the entire file silently not running.
#   DBT-70  check-schema-asset compared raw bytes. It failed on every Windows
#           run while reporting ZERO differing tables, which is the giveaway
#           that the difference was never in the data.
#
# NEITHER WAS VISIBLE TO CI, and that is the whole argument. The GitHub runner
# is Linux and checks out LF, so both were well-formed there while broken on
# the machine the work actually happens on. A green CI is evidence about the CI
# checkout, not about the repository.

# Normalise every text file to LF, in the repo and in the working tree. `auto`
# still lets git detect and leave binaries alone; the explicit rules below are
# belt-and-braces for the ones we cannot afford it to get wrong.
* text=auto eol=lf

# WINDOWS BATCH FILES MUST KEEP CRLF. cmd.exe parses them line by line and a
# lone LF can split a command mid-token. These are launchers under deprecated/
# and nothing runs them in CI, which is exactly why nobody would notice them
# breaking.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# ARCHIVES AND IMAGES - never inspected, never normalised. .crbl is a Cribl
# pack and .tgz is the packaged app; both are gzip (verified: they begin 1f 8b).
# If `text=auto` ever mis-detected one as text, normalising it would corrupt
# the archive silently, which is the one failure here that would not announce
# itself.
*.crbl binary
*.tgz  binary
*.png  binary
